Most creative tools start at execution. IdeaForge starts one step earlier — at the vague, unformed idea that has not yet earned a budget or a weekend.

# From spark to structure

Describe the idea in a sentence. The engine returns a product outline, a user-story map, and a feasibility read. Three language models are available, and the output is deliberately editable: the point is not to outsource taste, but to make the first draft of a product cheap enough to argue with.

# Prototype in the open

A template library generates a clickable UI prototype and the skeleton of an API document in one pass. A project can be published as an open collaboration, so other developers can suggest improvements, fork the branch, or join a structured critique. Community review is lightweight by design: a vote, a short expert note, and a clear record of what changed.

# Validate before you invest

Ideas fail quietly when they are tested in private. IdeaForge puts the premise in front of people early, when changing direction is still cheap. Teams leave the incubation stage with a prototype, a documented set of objections, and a decision they can defend.

# What comes next

The roadmap includes multimodal input — describe an idea with an image or a voice note — finer-grained collaboration permissions, and a deeper integration with CI/CD pipelines. IdeaForge is not trying to replace human creativity. It is trying to shorten the distance between a good idea and the first person who pays for it.

IdeaForge is used by independent developers and small studios, with 856 GitHub stars and a weekly cycle of new incubated projects.
